; Regression (2026-09-20): two top-level records of one compilation that share
; a name and disagree about their fields. A top-level define mangles without
; its module and without its package, so both collapse into ONE LLVM struct and
; every field access in the loser reads the other's layout — in packages/tscaly
; a bool on a ptr slot made `store i1 true` write into a pointer's low byte and
; the reader's return was DROPPED, false for the whole tree for four slices,
; with nothing said by planner or linker.
;
; ★No glob import here, on purpose: the glob-ambiguity gate already catches
; that spelling, and it is NOT how the shape occurs — each module simply uses
; its own record, which is invisible to every check there was.
;
; Declaring two such records is LEGAL and the language's answer is an explicit
; `use ns.Name` (glob_ambig_disambig, glob_disambig_field), so what is reported
; is the collision nobody disambiguated.
; xfail: error: record Thing is laid out twice with different fields

module record_collide_a
module record_collide_b

let a probe_a()
let b probe_b()
